Chapter: FactDisk

  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter ImportGate
  cites Foreword chapter SourceDefWire
  cites Foreword chapter FactLog

 A reader that turns the on-disk fact log into a FactBundle the import gate
 can read. DiskFacts writes the log and RepoProtocolPersist writes the
 source-definition records inside it; both live above the compiler, in
 codex.os and in apps/works, and the compiler cannot cite them. So the
 quotation gate could never reach the store: a quoted work had to travel
 beside the source in a text blob, because the one place that resolves
 quotations could not read the disk that holds the works.

 The on-disk layout it reads -- the sector size, the entry-header offsets,
 the superblock fields -- is Foreword chapter FactLog, the one definition
 DiskFacts shares; the kind-30 record inside an entry is decoded by
 Foreword chapter SourceDefWire, likewise shared with the writer. What is
 left here is the part that is genuinely this reader's: the effectful walk
 over the sectors, and the assembly of the results into a bundle.

 Reading a sector is a Device.Block effect. DiskFacts calls
 block-read-sector under a bare let because kernel quires are exempt from
 effect checking; foreword is not, so every function here that touches the
 disk declares the effect and binds the read with an act-bind. The effect
 stops at store-read-bundle, whose caller in the compiler already reads its
 input under an effect.

 What this does NOT re-verify is the log entry's own content hash;
 DiskFacts checks that on write. What it reads out is offered to the gate,
 and the gate re-hashes the work against the digest the quotation asked for
 before admitting a byte of it. A corrupt record therefore fails the gate's
 own integrity guard, not this reader's trust.

 We say:

Section: The Superblock

 Two candidate superblocks, and the reader trusts the one with the higher
 generation whose magic is intact. A sector with the wrong magic reports a
 generation below any real one, so it never wins; if neither is a
 superblock the log is empty and the walk covers nothing. Both generations
 and both log heads are read up front so the choice itself is pure.

  fd-gen : Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  fd-gen (sector) = act
   buf <- block-read-sector sector
   if fl-magic-ok buf then fl-sb-index-gen buf else 0 - 1
  end

  fd-log-head-of : Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  fd-log-head-of (sector) = act
   buf <- block-read-sector sector
   fl-sb-log-head buf
  end

  fd-log-head : [Device.Block] Integer
  fd-log-head = act
   g0 <- fd-gen 0
   g1 <- fd-gen 1
   lh0 <- fd-log-head-of 0
   lh1 <- fd-log-head-of 1
   if g0 < 0
    then (if g1 < 0 then fl-fact-log-start else lh1)
    else if g1 > g0 then lh1 else lh0
  end

Section: Reading An Entry's Content

  fd-copy-sector : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fd-copy-sector (src) (dst) (dst-off) (i) =
   if i >= fl-sector-size then 0
   else let w = poke-byte dst (dst-off + i) (peek-byte src i)
   in fd-copy-sector src dst dst-off (i + 1)

  fd-load-sectors : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  fd-load-sectors (start) (count) (dst) (i) =
   if i >= count then act 0 end
   else act
    src <- block-read-sector (start + i)
    let c = fd-copy-sector src dst (i * fl-sector-size) 0
    in fd-load-sectors start count dst (i + 1)
   end

  fd-entry-content : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] Text
  fd-entry-content (sector) (content-len) =
   let nsec = fl-sectors-for content-len
   in let buf = alloc-bytes (nsec * fl-sector-size)
   in act
    loaded <- fd-load-sectors sector nsec buf 0
    fl-text buf fl-header-size content-len
   end

Section: Decoding A Source Definition

 The kind-30 record's wire format -- the ninth-pipe rule, the
 length-prefixed content, the signature's hex -- is decoded by Foreword
 chapter SourceDefWire, the one place that also encodes it on the way in.
 A line that is not in that format (an old pointer-shaped record with no
 ninth pipe) decodes to None and is skipped; there is nothing to offer.

 The work becomes the Fact its own hash addresses -- sdw-hash over
 sdw-content, the digest a quotation names -- wearing the timestamp and
 author the signature covers, not the entry header's timestamp. An
 unsigned record is offered; a signed one is published, so the gate
 answers ImportUnsigned and ImportForged where each is the truth.

  fd-admit : FactBundle, Text -> FactBundle
  fd-admit (acc) (line) =
   when sdw-decode line
    is None -> acc
    is Just (w) -> fd-admit-work acc w

  fd-admit-work : FactBundle, SourceDefWire -> FactBundle
  fd-admit-work (acc) (w) =
   let fact = Fact {
     hash = w.sdw-hash,
     kind = DefinitionFact,
     content = w.sdw-content,
     author = w.sdw-author,
     timestamp = w.sdw-timestamp
   }
   in if list-length (w.sdw-signature) == 0
      then bundle-offer acc fact
      else bundle-publish acc (SignedFact { fact = fact, signature = w.sdw-signature, signer-fingerprint = w.sdw-signer })

Section: Walking The Log

 The walk yields a sector stride whether or not it yields a work, so one
 unreadable or over-long entry resynchronises on the next sector instead
 of ending the walk -- the truncation bug DiskFacts learned the hard way.
 Only kind-30 entries are decoded; every other kind strides past.

  fd-fold : Integer, Integer, FactBundle -> [Device.Block] FactBundle
  fd-fold (sector) (end-sec) (acc) =
   if sector >= end-sec then act acc end
   else act
    buf <- block-read-sector sector
    let clen = fl-entry-content-len buf
    in fd-fold-entry sector end-sec acc buf clen
   end

  fd-fold-entry : Integer, Integer, FactBundle, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] FactBundle
  fd-fold-entry (sector) (end-sec) (acc) (buf) (clen) =
   if clen == 0 then fd-fold (sector + 1) end-sec acc
   else let nsec = fl-sectors-for clen
   in if sector + nsec > end-sec then fd-fold (sector + 1) end-sec acc
   else let kind = fl-entry-kind buf
   in if kind == fl-kind-definition
      then act
       content <- fd-entry-content sector clen
       let acc2 = fd-admit acc content
       in fd-fold (sector + nsec) end-sec acc2
      end
      else fd-fold (sector + nsec) end-sec acc

 Everything the disk holds, added to the bundle already in hand. The
 compiler passes the bundle it built from the offered-works blob, so the
 store supplements the blob rather than replacing it: a quotation the
 blob satisfies still resolves, and one it does not is looked for on the
 disk.

  store-read-bundle : FactBundle -> [Device.Block] FactBundle
  store-read-bundle (base) = act
   lh <- fd-log-head
   fd-fold fl-fact-log-start lh base
  end
